\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2}{\ignorespaces (a)~Cut-and-paste on one arrangement (strong subadditivity; local picture on the quotient surface, Klein model). Cells carry the bits $(x_{AB},x_{BC})$; the outputs $\delta _B$ and $\delta _{ABC}$ bound the cells labelled $11$ and the cells $\neq  00$, resolved at the vertex. Any geodesic $g$ crosses the inputs at least as often as the outputs; integrating over $g$ with the measure $\mu $ gives the inequality for both chiral lengths simultaneously. (b)~Chart with the entangling point $p$ at $\infty $. All other ideal points lie in $[q_-,q_+]$. Geodesics of the class $\tau _p$ (gray) connect the two pieces adjacent to $p$; each crosses the cut arc ending at $p$ (red) exactly once. Their mass up to the horoball is $\log (1/\epsilon d_p)+o(1)$, independent of the cut. (c)~The resulting entropy vector: a finite graph whose min-cuts give the finite part $G$, plus one edge of weight $t_p(\epsilon )$ (dashed) joining the parties adjacent at each entangling point.}}{4}{figure.2}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:mechanism}{{2}{4}{(a)~Cut-and-paste on one arrangement (strong subadditivity; local picture on the quotient surface, Klein model). Cells carry the bits $(x_{AB},x_{BC})$; the outputs $\delta _B$ and $\delta _{ABC}$ bound the cells labelled $11$ and the cells $\neq 00$, resolved at the vertex. Any geodesic $g$ crosses the inputs at least as often as the outputs; integrating over $g$ with the measure $\mu $ gives the inequality for both chiral lengths simultaneously. (b)~Chart with the entangling point $p$ at $\infty $. All other ideal points lie in $[q_-,q_+]$. Geodesics of the class $\tau _p$ (gray) connect the two pieces adjacent to $p$; each crosses the cut arc ending at $p$ (red) exactly once. Their mass up to the horoball is $\log (1/\eps d_p)+o(1)$, independent of the cut. (c)~The resulting entropy vector: a finite graph whose min-cuts give the finite part $G$, plus one edge of weight $t_p(\eps )$ (dashed) joining the parties adjacent at each entangling point}{figure.2}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3}{\ignorespaces Smallest facet value $\min _QQ(\cdot )/\max _A|S_A|$ of $\mathcal  {C}_3,\mathcal  {C}_4,\mathcal  {C}_5$ (7, 20 and 372 facets) for 308 random configurations on pairs of pants (P) and one-holed tori (T) with two metrics of common marking, $N=3,4,5$ parties (asterisk: wider parameter ranges). $G$ and $F^\epsilon $ lie at rounding level; the single $F^\epsilon $ outlier has $\epsilon >\epsilon _1$ and is removed at smaller $\epsilon $, as Theorem~\ref {thm:main}(i) predicts. Two control functionals violate facets in most configurations.}}{4}{figure.3}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:numerics}{{3}{4}{Smallest facet value $\min _QQ(\cdot )/\max _A|S_A|$ of $\mathcal {C}_3,\mathcal {C}_4,\mathcal {C}_5$ (7, 20 and 372 facets) for 308 random configurations on pairs of pants (P) and one-holed tori (T) with two metrics of common marking, $N=3,4,5$ parties (asterisk: wider parameter ranges). $G$ and $F^\eps $ lie at rounding level; the single $F^\eps $ outlier has $\eps >\eps _1$ and is removed at smaller $\eps $, as Theorem~\ref {thm:main}(i) predicts. Two control functionals violate facets in most configurations}{figure.3}{}}
\newlabel{thm:matter}{{4}{4}{Matter}{theorem.4}{}}
